# Who is asked to review what.
#
# GitHub reads this file to decide whose review a pull request requests, and it only
# has an effect if the branch requires review from code owners, so today it is mostly a
# statement of intent: one maintainer, and everything is theirs.
#
# It is written out rather than left as a single `*` line because the interesting part
# is what happens when a second pair of hands arrives. The patterns below name the areas
# where a change is easy to make and hard to check, so that whoever understands that area
# is asked about it. Add specific patterns above the catch-all as they become true; until
# then they all resolve to the maintainer.

# Every file, unless something more specific below matches.
*                        @James-HoneyBadger

# The engines. A mistake here is a wrong answer in a language rather than a broken
# window, which is much harder to notice.
/native/src/lang/        @James-HoneyBadger
/native/src/scheme/      @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tests/golden/    @James-HoneyBadger

# The curriculum and the tools that drive it: what a learner sees, and the marking a
# teacher trusts. It is data, so nothing exercises it by accident.
/native/share/lessons.json @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/lessons/           @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tools/learn.cpp  @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tools/teach.cpp  @James-HoneyBadger

# The gates themselves. A change to these decides what "green" means for every other
# change, so it deserves a second look even when it looks harmless.
/native/tests/           @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tools/gates/     @James-HoneyBadger
/.github/workflows/      @James-HoneyBadger

# The documents that are read as statements about the product. Each of these has
# claimed something untrue at least once.
/README.md               @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/INDEX.md           @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/maintainer/        @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/project/history.md @James-HoneyBadger
/CONTRIBUTING.md         @James-HoneyBadger
/SECURITY.md             @James-HoneyBadger
